score_pct: 48.6
findings:
- finding_id: C1
  title: from_file_or_default swallows ALL errors including parse and permission failures
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase identified that from_file_or_default swallows all errors including
    parse/permission failures. TEA wrote config loading tests but none specifically
    test that parse errors propagate rather than falling back to defaults. The reviewer
    noted silent ignoring of unknown fields but not the broader error-swallowing in
    from_file_or_default.
- finding_id: I1
  title: CliError Display leaks internal details to stderr (CWE-209)
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: true
  caught_by: dev
  evidence: "Dev implemented CWE-209 safe Display on CliError \u2014 reviewer verified:\
    \ 'error.rs:40 ServerError Display suppresses the server body, showing only the\
    \ HTTP status code.' However, the Io(#[error(transparent)]) forwarding and eprintln\
    \ leakage were not flagged \u2014 the fix is partial. TEA also wrote CWE-209 tests\
    \ for server error display."
- finding_id: I2
  title: "SiemClient.tenant_id uses raw String \u2014 header injection risk (CWE-113)"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: tea
  evidence: TEA wrote CWE-113 header injection prevention tests for tenant ID with
    newlines. Dev implemented stripping of \r\n from tenant values in client.rs:23,29.
    However, the type still uses raw String rather than axiathon-core's validated
    TenantId, so the root cause (bypassing type-safe tenant isolation) was not addressed.
- finding_id: I3
  title: Dependencies use direct version strings instead of workspace = true
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: 'Reviewer flagged as HIGH: ''Several dependencies are declared inline
    with version strings instead of referencing workspace declarations: clap_complete,
    comfy-table, csv, dirs, reqwest, toml. This defeats the purpose of workspace-level
    dependency management.'''
- finding_id: I4
  title: "Config token field silently ignored \u2014 secret left on disk"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: Reviewer noted 'RawConfig silently ignores unknown fields' and suggested
    deny_unknown_fields or warning, but framed it as 'a design decision, not a bug.'
    The specific scenario of a user putting token in config believing they're authenticated
    was not flagged. The reviewer verified 'tokens placed in config file are silently
    ignored rather than loaded' as a positive, missing that silent ignoring IS the
    problem.
- finding_id: I5
  title: "Vacuously true test \u2014 assertion is None || true"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer flagged as MEDIUM: 'assert!(result.is_ok() || result.is_err(),\
    \ ...) This assertion is always true (is_ok() || is_err() covers all cases of\
    \ Result). It proves nothing.' Also flagged the token test: '!client.has_token()\
    \ || { true } \u2014 the || { true } branch means this assertion can never fail.'"
- finding_id: I6
  title: "Test with zero assertions \u2014 only checks non-panic"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase flagged the zero-assertion test that only checks non-panic with
    'let _ = config'. The reviewer caught vacuous assertions in siem_client tests
    but did not identify this specific config test with no assertions.
